//? Title: Profile a TensorFlow CPU graph across thread settings
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

\abstract(
Measure untraced execution for two independent CPU matrix products, then
capture a separate trace and export it for Chrome or Perfetto.
)
\toc

The graph computes \(XY+YX\) for two fixed 256-by-256 matrices. The
products can run concurrently. Intra-op threads are available to split
work within an operation, while inter-op threads schedule independent
operations. The benchmark creates a separate session for each thread
configuration.

After five warm-up calls, measure the median of 24 untraced calls. Each
call includes the adapter's tensor copies. Request full runtime metadata
only for a separate invocation so profiling overhead stays out of the
timing samples. The trace uses complete events with a device group,
thread ID, start time, and duration. Both products must appear.

Compile Datamunge with `-DDATAMUNGE_ENABLE_TENSORFLOW=ON` and set
`CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` to a TensorFlow C++ SDK. Open the generated
`build/runtime_profile/runtime_*.json` files in Chrome Trace Viewer
or Perfetto to inspect operation overlap.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  if (!n::saved_model_runtime_available()) {
    std::println("Runtime profiling requires DATAMUNGE_ENABLE_TENSORFLOW.");
    return 0;
  }
  constexpr int side = 256;
  std::vector<float> a(side*side), b(side*side);
  for (int i = 0; i < side; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < side; ++j) {
      a[i*side+j] = ((i*3+j*5)%47-23)*.015625f;
      b[i*side+j] = ((i*7+j*11)%53-26)*.015625f;
    }
  const n::NamedTensors feeds{{"x",n::from_vector(a,{side,side})},{"y",n::from_vector(b,{side,side})}};
  const std::vector<std::string> fetches{"sum_products"};
  const std::vector<n::NamedTensors> batches{feeds};
  const std::string folder = "build/runtime_profile/";
  std::filesystem::create_directories(folder);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  std::vector<double> medians;
  n::NamedTensors archive = feeds;
  std::println("| Intra-op threads | Inter-op threads | Median µs | Trace events | Max reference error |");
  std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
  for (const auto [intra, inter] : std::array{std::pair{1,1},std::pair{2,2},std::pair{4,2}}) {
    const n::GraphSession session("fixtures/runtime_products.pb", n::GraphOptimization::Grappler,
                                   intra, inter);
    const auto timing = n::benchmark_inference(
        [&](const auto& batch) { return session.run(batch, fetches); }, batches, 1, 5, 24);
    std::vector<n::GraphTraceEvent> events;
    const auto output = session.run(feeds, fetches, nullptr, &events).front().second;
    if (output.shape() != n::Shape{side,side}) throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected result shape");
    const auto actual = output.to_vector<float>();
    double max_error = 0.;
    for (int i = 0; i < side; ++i) {
      const int j = (i*13+9)%side;
      double expected = 0.;
      for (int k = 0; k < side; ++k)
        expected += double(a[i*side+k])*b[k*side+j] + double(b[i*side+k])*a[k*side+j];
      max_error = std::max(max_error,std::abs(expected-actual[i*side+j]));
    }
    for (const auto* name : {"product_xy", "product_yx"})
      if (std::ranges::count(events, name, &n::GraphTraceEvent::name) != 1)
        throw std::runtime_error("Trace did not include both CPU products");
    if (!(max_error <= 1e-5)) throw std::runtime_error("Traced output differs from scalar reference");
    const auto suffix = std::to_string(intra)+"_"+std::to_string(inter);
    n::save_graph_trace(folder+"runtime_"+suffix+".json", events);
    archive.emplace_back("out_"+suffix,output);
    medians.push_back(timing.median_us);
    std::println("| {} | {} | {:.1f} | {} | {:.3g} |",
                 intra,inter,timing.median_us,events.size(),max_error);
  }

  n::save_npz(folder+"products.npz",archive);
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points({1.,2.,3.},medians,"Untraced median",{37,99,235},6)
      .title("Warmed CPU graph execution")
      .x_label("1: 1/1 threads · 2: 2/2 threads · 3: 4/2 threads").y_label("Microseconds")
      .save_svg(folder+"latency.svg");
  std::filesystem::copy_file(folder+"latency.svg","figures/runtime_profile.svg",
                             std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
  std::println("");
  std::println("\\image(figures/runtime_profile.svg)");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Intra-op threads | Inter-op threads | Median µs | Trace events | Max reference error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | 352.9 | 7 | 0 |
| 2 | 2 | 224.0 | 7 | 0 |
| 4 | 2 | 227.9 | 7 | 0 |

\image(figures/runtime_profile.svg)
// result_end

Timing may improve or worsen as the thread counts increase. The trace
and untraced timings answer different questions about the same graph.

> Reference

- [Perfetto documentation, importing Chrome JSON traces|https://perfetto.dev/docs/getting-started/other-formats].
